+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdMXk7PRfGDMMBsNC46Kc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The scheduler is implemented, verified, and all three commands pass.\n\n## What I built\n\n**`src/scheduler.ts`** — the solver, in three layers:\n\n1. **Setup** — cycle detection over the prerequisite graph (DFS, back edge ⇒ `PrerequisiteCycleError` carrying the offending path), then a normalized problem: resolved prerequisite indices, room-preference indices, and an `impossible` flag for events no ordering can save (no room large enough, longer than the window, prerequisite missing from the batch). Impossibility propagates to dependents.\n\n2. **Construction** — place events one at a time in a topological order. For each, the earliest legal start is `max(windowStart, max(prereq.end + gap))`, and a linear scan of the room's timeline finds the first hole that keeps `gap` clear on both sides. Preferred rooms are taken whenever they yield any feasible slot (rule 5).\n\n3. **Search** — a single greedy pass can't maximize placement, so the order is the search variable. Nine structurally different priority vectors (longest-first, most-constrained-first, critical-path, dependent-count, …) are each run against both preference policies and two room-choice rules, then a large-neighbourhood search repeatedly promotes an event that missed out and re-runs the construction, keeping any improvement. Solutions are ranked by events scheduled → preferences honoured → compactness. It stops early once a provable upper bound is reached, and is bounded by both an iteration count and a 1.5 s deadline. A seeded PRNG keeps output deterministic.\n\n`src/index.ts` exposes `schedule` as a named export, a default export, a `Scheduler` class, and a `createScheduler` factory.\n\n## Verification\n\nBeyond the 28 tests in `tests/scheduler.test.ts` (each result checked against every rule by a shared validator), I fuzzed against a **brute-force optimal solver** — max over all permutations × room-assignments of the leftmost placement, which is provably the true optimum. Across **1,600 small random instances** the search matched the optimum exactly, zero shortfalls. A second fuzzer validated **1,200 larger instances** (up to 120 events, 6 rooms, zero gaps, zero durations, missing prerequisites); slowest solve was 33 ms.\n\nThat second fuzzer found a real bug worth flagging: with `gap: 0`, a zero-duration event can legitimately start at the same instant another event starts in the same room. The placement is legal, but the room's timeline became ambiguous to order, so a validator sorting by `startTime` could read it as an overlap. My first fix tightened placement and cost schedulability, so I reverted it — the output now emits each room's own entries in timeline order while leaving everything else in input order. `tests/scheduler.test.ts:138` is a regression test for it; I confirmed it fails with the fix disabled.\n\nOne judgement call worth your review: an event whose prerequisite id isn't in the batch is treated as **unschedulable** rather than having the prerequisite ignored, matching the rule that an event whose prerequisite goes unscheduled also goes unscheduled. The alternative reading — ignore unknown ids — is defensible; say the word and it's a one-line change in `buildProblem`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":262,"cache_read_input_tokens":88176,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":262},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b76a2cb-7819-464e-b7d1-dad16ea130c1","uuid":"8e3338fd-3650-4962-b701-71c55d2e7065","request_id":"req_011CdMXk6DUr2PbXcVpMFftC"}
